The movement flared to life during the brutal Arctic winter of 1982 in the storm-battered towers of the Shetland and Hebridean outposts. Marooned for months in perpetual twilight, a rogue network of civilian lighthouse keepers modified their emergency radio transmitters to broadcast pirate dance shows across the North Atlantic shipping corridors. Defying maritime regulations, they raided their lockers to craft an outlandish look: grease-stained wool oilskins worn over ruffled pirate cravats, gold lamé waistcoats, sea-boots polished with automotive glitter, and cobalt frostbite-resistant cheek paint. Their light towers became vertical dance halls where keepers threw wild two-man parties to stave off cabin fever, transmitting their manic "light-struts" to passing trawler crews who eagerly tuned their navigation receivers to the unauthorized frequencies, dancing on freezing decks in the pitch black.

